// The same question twice: once written out, once as a query. Demonstrates:
//   • `?-`, a rule that brings its own head declaration — no `.out` section,
//     no `.decl`, no column types
//   • that the two forms are the same program: `Payroll` and `PayrollQ` derive
//     identical rows, and the second is what the first desugars to
//   • two `?-` rules over one head, which is how a query writes a union
//
// The types of a query's columns are recovered by inference when something
// needs them — `flow-ts inspect examples/payroll.dl` shows them.
//
// Run it:
//     node packages/cli/dist/bin.js -p examples/payroll.dl -f examples

.in
.decl Person(id: number, name: string, dept: string)
.input Person2.csv

.decl Salary(id: number, amount: number)
.input Salary.csv

.out
.decl Payroll(dept: string, total: number)

.rule
Payroll(d, sum(s)) :- Person(i, n, d), Salary(i, s).

// The same rule, without the four lines above it.
?- PayrollQ(d, sum(s)) :- Person(i, n, d), Salary(i, s).

// Two rules, one head: everyone in engineering or operations.
?- Core(n) :- Person(i, n, "eng").
?- Core(n) :- Person(i, n, "ops").

// And with no head at all — a bare goal reports the variables its body binds,
// in order, under a name generated for it (`Query1` here). Note that it reports
// *every* bound variable, `i` included: a join key has to be named to do its
// job, so it comes back too. `_` only drops a column nothing needs — when you
// want to choose the columns, name the head, as `Core` does above.
?- Person(i, n, d), Salary(i, s), s > 100.
